    stem: "Silver metal reacts with hydrogen sulfide (H₂S) to form solid silver sulfide (Ag₂S) according to the following balanced equation. 2Ag + H₂S → Ag₂S + H₂ When a silver teapot was exposed to H₂S in moist air over a period of time, 10.8 g of Ag was converted to Ag₂S which appeared as a black tarnish on the surface of the teapot.",
    questionText: "What is the ratio of moles of Ag to moles of H₂ in the equation above? How many moles of H₂ are produced when 10.8 g of Ag react in this way? What volume does this amount of H₂ occupy at s.t.p.?",
